        owner.setRegistrationDate(LocalDate.now());
    }
    ownerRepository.save(owner);
}
```

and reverted `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` to the thin pass-through it was, dropping the now-unused `LocalDate` import there.

**Why this is correct for the update path too:** `updateOwner` loads the existing entity and never clears `registrationDate`, so a previously-dated owner arrives at `saveOwner` with a non-null date and the guard leaves it untouched. Only genuine creates (where the mapped-from-DTO entity has a null date and none was supplied) get today's date. This also means any future create path (e.g. a V2 endpoint) inherits the default for free through the single `saveOwner` choke point.

I placed the new `java.time.LocalDate` import with the existing `java.util.*` group at the bottom to match the file's import ordering.

